---
import Sheet from "../layouts/Sheet.astro";
import bundle from "../data/bundle.json";

const live = bundle.companies.filter((c) => !c.missing);
const checked = live.reduce((n, c) => n + (c.results.reconciliation?.citations_checked ?? 0), 0);
const matched = live.reduce((n, c) => n + (c.results.reconciliation?.citations_matched ?? 0), 0);
const rate = checked ? (matched / checked) * 100 : 0;
const pill = { text: `${rate.toFixed(0)}% of citations verified`, tone: rate > 95 ? "good" : "warn" };

const split = live.reduce((acc, c) => {
  const s = c.results.evidence?.verification_split ?? {};
  for (const k of ["prose", "structured", "derived"]) acc[k] = (acc[k] ?? 0) + (s[k] ?? 0);
  return acc;
}, {});
const totalClaims = Object.values(split).reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) || 1;
---

<Sheet title="Integrity" system="09 · every citation matched, every gap named" sheet="09" pill={pill}>
  <div class="grid four">
    <div class="stat"><div class="k">{checked}</div><div class="v">citations checked</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="k">{matched}</div><div class="v">matched to their claim</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="k">{checked - matched}</div><div class="v">failed, and dropped their lens</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="k">{totalClaims}</div><div class="v">claims in the evidence store</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h2>How verification splits three ways</h2>
    <p class="sub">the distinction matters, and collapsing it would be the loophole</p>
    <div class="grid two">
      <div>
        <div class="splitrow"><b>prose</b> <span class="muted">{split.prose ?? 0} claims</span>
          <span class="bartrack"><span class="barfill" style={`width:${((split.prose ?? 0) / totalClaims) * 100}%;background:var(--series-1)`}></span></span></div>
        <p class="secondary">A human wrote sentences, a model quoted one, and the quote is string-matched against the document it came from. This is where fabrication is possible and where matching earns its keep — a range assembled from two different sentences reads perfectly and is not what the company said.</p>
        <div class="splitrow"><b>structured</b> <span class="muted">{split.structured ?? 0} claims</span>
          <span class="bartrack"><span class="barfill" style={`width:${((split.structured ?? 0) / totalClaims) * 100}%;background:var(--series-3)`}></span></span></div>
        <p class="secondary">The quote is the tagged fact itself, rendered by a source adapter from a typed response. There is no prose to match, and that is not a loophole: the value came from the adapter and never from a model.</p>
        <div class="splitrow"><b>derived</b> <span class="muted">{split.derived ?? 0} claims</span>
          <span class="bartrack"><span class="barfill" style={`width:${((split.derived ?? 0) / totalClaims) * 100}%;background:var(--series-2)`}></span></span></div>
        <p class="secondary">Figures the pipeline computed. Deliberately in neither set: a derived number is only as good as its inputs and must be justified by the claims underneath it rather than by its own existence.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 style="font-size:13px;margin:0 0 8px">Point-in-time enforcement</h3>
        <p class="secondary">
          Two independent locks. Each adapter filters on the filing date, and the chain re-checks
          everything returned — the second lock catches an adapter that forgets, which is the bug
          the guard exists for. A test trips it with a document filed one day after the lock date.
          The retrieval window for web research is bounded at the API itself, so an article
          published after the lock date cannot enter the result set at all rather than being
          filtered afterwards. The market adapter refuses a historical as-of date outright, because
          serving today's consensus for a past quarter is lookahead that would surface as skill.
        </p>
        <h3 style="font-size:13px;margin:14px 0 8px">News is deliberately not evidence</h3>
        <p class="secondary">
          We hold a snippet rather than the article, so matching a quote against the snippet would
          be circular. Coverage stays on the dossier, feeds perception only, and no lens can cite
          it. Retrieved <em>research</em> is different: its full text is written to disk as a
          document, so a quote against it is verified exactly like a quote from an 8-K.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  {live.map((c) => (
    <div class="panel">
      <h2>{c.results.header.company} <span class="muted">· {c.ticker}</span></h2>
      <p class="sub">which source answered which request, and what acquisition skipped</p>
      <div class="scroll">
        <table>
          <tr><th>request</th><th>answered by</th></tr>
          {Object.entries(c.results.integrity?.answered_by ?? {}).map(([req, src]) => (
            <tr><td class="mono" style="font-size:12px">{req}</td>
              <td>{src ? <b>{src}</b> : <span class="muted">nothing answered</span>}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </table>
      </div>
      {(c.results.integrity?.methods_no_source_answered ?? []).length > 0 && (
        <p class="secondary" style="font-size:12.5px">
          <b>No source answers:</b> <span class="mono">{c.results.integrity.methods_no_source_answered.join(", ")}</span>.
          These are capabilities this system does not have, named rather than inferred from silence.
        </p>
      )}
      {(c.results.dossier?.skipped ?? []).length === 0
        ? <p class="secondary" style="font-size:12.5px"><b>Acquisition skipped nothing.</b> Every ranked target was reached within budget.</p>
        : <ul class="skips">{c.results.dossier.skipped.map((s) => <li><b>{s.acquirer}</b>: {s.skipped} skipped — {s.reason}</li>)}</ul>}
      {(c.results.evidence?.dropped ?? []).length > 0 && (
        <details>
          <summary>{c.results.evidence.dropped.length} items dropped before reaching the store</summary>
          <table>
            <tr><th>label</th><th>reason</th></tr>
            {c.results.evidence.dropped.slice(0, 40).map((d) => (
              <tr><td>{d.label ?? "—"}</td><td class="muted">{d.reason}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </table>
        </details>
      )}
    </div>
  ))}
</Sheet>

<style>
  .stat { background: var(--surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 15px 17px; }
  .stat .k { font-size: 26px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -.02em; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .stat .v { color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 12px; margin-top: 2px; }
  .splitrow { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 90px 90px 1fr; align-items: center; gap: 10px; font-size: 13px; margin-top: 12px; }
  .bartrack { display: block; background: var(--surface-2); border-radius: 4px; height: 9px; overflow: hidden; }
  .barfill { display: block; height: 100%; border-radius: 4px; }
  .secondary { font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--text-secondary); margin: 5px 0 0; }
  .skips { font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--text-secondary); }
  details { margin-top: 12px; }
  summary { cursor: pointer; font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--text-muted); }
</style>
